$DIR/test.p:13:34: []byte(s) is not permitted in Plutonium (the copy would belong to no one; use strings.Bytes, or copy into a buffer)
    Plutonium has no []byte(s) or []rune(s) for a string s. In Go the conversion
    allocates a new slice and the garbage collector frees it later. A Plutonium
    slice is a view of an array that something else owns, so a copy of a string
    needs an owner to free it.
    strings.Bytes(s) and strings.Runes(s) from pluto/strings return a vec.Vec
    that owns the copy and frees it when it is dropped; v.Slice() lends the
    elements as a slice. To fill a buffer of your own without allocating, use
    copy(buf[:], s), which works from a string into a []byte. To visit the runes
    of a string, range over it, and to count them, use
    utf8.RuneCountInString(s).
    Go allocates the copy behind the program's back and leaves the garbage
    collector to free it. Plutonium never allocates unless the code says so, and
    every allocation has an owner, so that every cost is visible and the same
    code can run inside a kernel.
    To keep a copy that you own, in a vec.Vec[byte] that frees it when it is
    dropped:
        import "pluto/strings"

        b := strings.Bytes(s)
        // b.Slice() is the []byte
    To copy into a buffer of your own without allocating:
        var buf [64]byte
        n := copy(buf[:], s)
        b := buf[:n]
    (see "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24)
    (run "pluto explain E2016" for a longer explanation)
